.TH HD-SECBOOT-CHECK 8 "October 2026" "hd-base" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-secboot-check \- check that Secure Boot is on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-secboot-check
.RB [ \-q ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-secboot-check
checks that the system booted with Secure Boot on, and says so.
When it is off, it says why: it is turned off in the setup menu of the
firmware, the firmware is in setup mode and has no platform key, the
firmware does not support Secure Boot, or the system was not booted with
UEFI.
See
.BR hd-secboot (7)
for the keys, and
.BR hd-secboot-enroll (8)
to enroll them.
.PP
It reads the EFI variables
.B SecureBoot
and
.B SetupMode
in
.IR /sys/firmware/efi/efivars ,
which every user can read, so it does not need to run as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Say nothing, only set the exit status.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when Secure Boot is on, 1 when it is not, 2 on wrong arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sys/firmware/efi/efivars
The EFI variables, among them SecureBoot and SetupMode.
.SH EXAMPLES
In a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
if ! hd-secboot-check \-q; then
    echo "Secure Boot is off."
fi
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-secboot (7),
.BR hd-secboot-enroll (8),
.BR hd-report (8)
